  <constraints>
    <constraint type="performance">Impact propagation traverses up to 4 hops. The 1.5 exponent decay ensures natural falloff: 1-hop always flagged for strong edges, 4-hop rarely flagged. Must not cause flag storms — the decay formula is critical.</constraint>
    <constraint type="idempotency">Multiple paths to the same downstream node use the HIGHEST impact score. Do not double-flag — take max score across all paths.</constraint>
    <constraint type="ordering">Impact propagation runs AFTER compilation (article changes written) but BEFORE graph-sync (so flagged status is included in the Memgraph sync).</constraint>
    <constraint type="dual-update">Flagged status must be updated in BOTH places: Memgraph (via Cypher SET) AND wiki article frontmatter (on disk). Wiki is source of truth; Memgraph is the query accelerator.</constraint>
    <constraint type="all-compilations">Engine must support all four compilation contexts: story, epic, deployment, and pre-dev. Pre-dev compilation (Epic 4) is built later, but the engine API must accommodate it now.</constraint>
    <constraint type="dependency">This story depends on Epic 2 (story compilation provides the primary integration point) and Story 1.5 (graph-sync for Memgraph traversal). Also depends on Story 3.1 and 3.3 for epic and deployment integration points.</constraint>
    <constraint type="supersession">When a node is superseded, impact propagation triggers on the SUPERSEDING (new) node, not the superseded (old) node. The old node is being replaced — downstream consumers need to know about the new version.</constraint>
  </constraints>

